"""Default CareBridge AI knowledge base. Platform entries describe what the software really does.
Entries marked "(template)" are safeguarding guidance to ADAPT to your organization's own policy and local law."""

G = "CareBridge guide"
S = "Security guide"
T = "Safeguarding guidance (template)"

ENTRIES = [
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ getting started
    ("What is CareBridge AI", G, "CareBridge AI is a platform that connects children's needs with the people and resources that can meet them. Organizations (child-care homes) post what they need, donors and volunteers offer help, and AI suggests the best matches. An administrator reviews and approves every important decision, so people stay in control. Everything is recorded so the work is transparent."),
    ("The five-stage workflow explained simply", G, "The workflow has five stages. 1 Data input: children, organizations, donors, volunteers and resources are recorded. 2 AI processing: the system predicts future needs, flags suspicious donations, suggests donors, volunteers and resources, and gives career guidance. 3 Decision: the AI assistant answers questions and the administrator reviews and approves. 4 Action: donors and volunteers are connected, resources are allocated, guidance is given. 5 Outcomes: children receive timely support, resources are used well, and everything stays transparent."),
    ("User roles and what each can do", G, "There are four roles. An organization manages its own children, needs, volunteers and resources. A donor records interests and gifts and sees needs from verified organizations. A volunteer records skills and availability. An administrator is the central coordinator who sees and manages all organizations, needs, donors, volunteers and resources, reviews AI suggestions, and can see the audit trail."),
    ("How to register an organization", G, "Choose Sign up, select the organization role, and enter your organization name, contact details, email and a password of at least 12 characters. After signing up you can add needs, children, volunteers and resources, but donors and volunteers cannot see your needs until an administrator verifies your organization."),
    ("What organization verification means", G, "Verification is an administrator's check that an organization is real and trustworthy. Until an organization is verified, its needs stay hidden from donors and volunteers and it is shown as unverified. Administrators verify organizations on the Organizations page or from the Review queue, and every verification is written to the audit trail."),
    ("How donors register and set their interests", G, "Donors sign up with the donor role and record what they like to support, for example education, food or medicine. These interests are what the AI uses to suggest needs and organizations for the donor, so the more specific the interests, the better the suggestions. Donors see only needs from verified organizations."),
    ("How volunteers register skills and availability", G, "Volunteers sign up with the volunteer role and record their skills, such as teaching or cooking, and when they are available, such as weekends or evenings. The AI compares skills and availability with open needs to suggest where a volunteer can help most. A volunteer who lists no availability is ranked lower and flagged for confirmation."),
    ("How administrator accounts are created", G, "The first administrator can sign up at the administrator sign-up page without an invite code while no administrator exists. After that, new administrators are added by an existing administrator on the Administrators page, or by using the invite code set in the server settings. Administrators can also be created with the create_admin script. Only administrator accounts see the Administration menu."),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ needs and children
    ("How to add a need", G, "On the Needs page choose Add need and enter a category such as books, food or medicine, the quantity required, and the urgency. New needs start as open. Keep needs specific and small: clear requests are fulfilled faster than long vague lists. Administrators can edit or delete needs, but a need that already has donations can only be closed."),
    ("Urgency levels and the AI priority score", G, "Urgency can be critical, high, medium or low. The AI priority score from 0 to 1 combines three things: the urgency level (65 percent), how long the need has been open with a full score at 30 days (20 percent), and the quantity requested (15 percent). Critical needs that have waited a long time therefore rise to the top of the administrator's list."),
    ("Need statuses open pending fulfilled closed", G, "A need is open when it is waiting for help, pending when help is on the way, fulfilled when the request has been met, and closed when it is no longer needed. Only open and pending needs receive AI suggestions. A need is marked fulfilled automatically when the delivered resources cover its full quantity."),
    ("Adding child records and consent", G, "Organizations record each child's age, interests, skills and academic performance so that needs and guidance can be planned. Record only what is necessary, get consent where required by your policy and local law, and never put sensitive details in free text that is not needed. Children's data is never shown to donors or volunteers."),
    ("Who can see a child's record", G, "A child's record can be seen by the administrators and by the organization that cares for the child. Donors and volunteers cannot see children's records. Access is controlled by roles, and administrator actions are recorded in the audit trail."),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ donations and fraud
    ("How to record a donation", G, "Open Donations and record the donor, the need it supports, and either an amount or a description of the in-kind gift such as books or meals. Recording every gift as it arrives keeps progress accurate and lets the fraud checks work. Donations that fully cover a need can lead to the need being marked fulfilled."),
    ("How fraud and anomaly detection works", G, "The system checks each donation against simple rules and gives it an anomaly score from 0 to 1. A donation with no donor linked adds 0.3. A first gift above 50,000 adds 0.4. An amount at least five times the donor's average adds 0.5. Four or more gifts within one hour add 0.4. Donations that trigger a rule appear as flags. The AI only flags; an administrator decides."),
    ("How to review a suspicious donation flag", G, "Open the flag from the Review queue or the Fraud flags page and read the reasons the system gave. Check the donor and the amount, contact the donor if needed, then choose Confirm fraud or False alarm. The decision, your name and the time are saved in the audit trail. A flag is a question, not an accusation."),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ predictions and suggestions
    ("How need alerts predict problems early", G, "Need alerts look at open needs and warn the administrator early. A high-urgency need that is still open adds 0.5. A need open longer than its limit adds 0.4, where the limit is 2 days for high urgency, 5 days for medium, 10 for low and 7 otherwise. A request of 50 units or more adds 0.3. Alerts are reviewed as action taken or dismissed."),
    ("How the demand forecast works", G, "The forecast predicts how many needs and units each category will see in the next 30 days. Needs are grouped into 30-day periods and a damped-trend smoothing model is fitted for each category. The result is compared with the stock on hand and the donors and volunteers whose interests match, so a likely shortage is visible before requests arrive. With fewer than three periods of history the forecast uses a recent average and is marked low confidence."),
    ("How donor suggestions work", G, "For each open need the AI ranks donors by fit. Fit combines how well the donor's interests match the need (55 percent, using category match and text similarity), the donor's giving history for similar needs (20 percent), how recently they gave (10 percent) and how often administrators approved earlier suggestions for them (15 percent). Priority is half the need's urgency and half the donor fit. Rejected donors are not suggested again for the same need."),
    ("How the AI learns from administrator decisions", G, "Donor and volunteer suggestions start as a transparent weighted blend. Once at least 20 suggestions have been approved or rejected, with both kinds present, a logistic regression model is trained on those decisions and averaged with the blend. This way the ranking slowly adapts to what administrators actually approve. The Overview page shows how many decisions the model has seen."),
    ("How volunteer suggestions work", G, "For each open need the AI ranks volunteers. Fit combines how well their skills match the need (45 percent), their availability (15 percent), confirmed earlier help with similar needs (15 percent), whether they already belong to the organization (10 percent) and how often administrators approved earlier suggestions (15 percent). Volunteers with no stated availability are flagged so they can be confirmed first."),
    ("How resource allocation suggestions work", G, "Resources are materials, services or funds with a category and a quantity. For an open need the AI scores each available resource by category relevance (60 percent), how much of the remaining need it can cover (30 percent) and whether it is held by the same organization (10 percent). An administrator approves the proposal, which reserves the stock, then marks it delivered. Cancelling an approved allocation returns the stock."),
    ("How the organization health score works", G, "Each organization gets a score from 0 to 100 made of six parts: verification (10), responsiveness to stale or unmatched urgent needs (25), fulfilment of needs (20), funding momentum over the last 30 days (15), donor diversity (15) and completeness of child profiles (15). Grades are Thriving at 80 and above, Healthy at 60, Needs attention at 40 and At risk below that. Each weakness comes with a concrete suggestion."),
    ("How the AI suggestion hub works", G, "The suggestion hub lets an administrator pick any organization, need, donor or volunteer and see the best related suggestions. A need shows the best donors, volunteers, resources and partner organizations. A donor or volunteer shows the best needs and organizations. An organization shows its needs in priority order, the best donors, volunteers and resources, its health score and the forecast. Every suggestion has a score and reasons."),
    ("What fit urgency priority and score mean", G, "Fit says how well a donor, volunteer or resource suits a need, from 0 to 1. Urgency says how pressing the need is. Priority combines the two so that urgent needs with a good match come first. A higher number is better, but every number comes with plain-language reasons, and an administrator makes the final decision."),
    ("How career guidance works", G, "Career guidance compares a child's profile, such as interests, skills and academic performance, with many known profiles and averages the similarity for each career path. The child's stated interest is placed first and the top three paths are shown. A counsellor or administrator records whether the guidance was useful, and that feedback is saved. The guidance is a starting point for a conversation, never a decision about the child."),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ administrator work
    ("What the review queue is", G, "The review queue is the one place where everything the AI hands to a person waits for a decision: suspicious donations, predicted needs, organization verification, resource allocations, donor and volunteer matches, and donor and volunteer suggestions. Each item shows why it was raised and has clear buttons. Every decision is saved with the administrator's name in the audit trail."),
    ("How to run the AI scans", G, "On the Administration page press Run all AI scans. One click looks for predicted needs, scans donations for suspicious patterns, creates donor and volunteer suggestions for the most urgent open needs and proposes resource allocations. The results appear in the review queue. Running the scans daily keeps suggestions fresh."),
    ("How administrators manage donors volunteers and organizations", G, "The administrator is the central coordinator. The All donors, All volunteers and Organizations pages list every record across all organizations with search and filters. Administrators can add, edit, link to an organization and delete records. Donors and volunteers who have recorded gifts, matches or a sign-in account are kept for the audit trail instead of being deleted."),
    ("What the audit trail records", G, "The audit trail records sign-ins and failed sign-ins, account lockouts, password and two-step verification changes, and every administrator decision such as verifying an organization, reviewing a flag or approving a suggestion. Each entry shows the time, who did it, what happened and the network address. Entries are append-only: nobody can edit or delete them, which builds trust."),
    ("How to mark resources as delivered", G, "After an allocation is approved, the resource is handed to the organization. Open Resources, then Allocations, and choose Mark delivered. When the delivered quantity covers the need, the need becomes fulfilled automatically. If the plan changes, choose Cancel and the stock returns to the resource."),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ security
    ("Password rules and changing your password", S, "Passwords must have at least 12 characters, mix at least three kinds of characters (upper case, lower case, numbers, symbols), use several different characters, and must not contain the name from your email address. Change your password in Settings under Security by entering the current password first. Changing it signs out every other device."),
    ("Account lockout after wrong passwords", S, "After 5 wrong passwords or wrong two-step codes in a row the account is locked for 15 minutes and the message says how long to wait. A correct sign-in resets the counter. There is also a limit on how many attempts one device can make in a few minutes. Lockouts are written to the audit trail so administrators can notice attacks."),
    ("How to set up two-step verification", S, "Administrators can turn on two-step verification in Settings under Security. Install an authenticator app, add an account using the setup key shown, then type the 6-digit code to switch it on. From then on every administrator sign-in needs the password and a fresh code. The secret is stored encrypted and each code can be used only once."),
    ("Recovery codes and lost phone for two-step verification", S, "When two-step verification is turned on you receive 8 one-time recovery codes, shown only once. Save them somewhere safe. If you lose your phone, enter a recovery code instead of the 6-digit code; each recovery code works one time. You can create a new set in Settings after confirming your password. If you have no codes left, another administrator must help you."),
    ("How to sign out of all devices", S, "If you lost a phone or used a shared computer, open Settings, Security and choose Sign out of all other devices. Every other session ends immediately while the current one continues. Changing your password does the same."),
    ("How to reset a forgotten password", S, "Choose Forgot password and enter your email. If email delivery is set up, a one-time reset code is sent that expires in 30 minutes. If email is not configured, ask an administrator to issue a one-time reset code for you; it is shown once, expires in 30 minutes and works one time. After resetting, sign in with the new password."),
    ("Privacy and data protection principles", S, "CareBridge AI follows a few privacy rules. Collect only the data you need. Show each person only what their role requires. Never show children's records to donors or volunteers. Keep passwords hashed and two-step secrets encrypted. Record administrator actions in an audit trail. Do not sell or share personal data. Adapt retention periods to your local law."),
    ("Why sign-in says too many attempts", S, "Too many attempts means the safety limit was reached: either the account is locked for 15 minutes after repeated wrong passwords, or one device made too many sign-in requests in a few minutes. Wait for the time shown and try again. If you forgot your password use Forgot password instead of guessing."),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ the assistant
    ("How the AI assistant answers questions", G, "The assistant searches the approved knowledge base using three methods together: word matching, typo-tolerant text matching and meaning-based vectors. It fuses and re-ranks the results and shows the best passages with a confidence level. For questions about current needs, donors or organization health it also reads the live database, limited to what your role may see. Answers always show their sources."),
    ("Why the assistant says it is not confident", G, "When the best passage does not match the question well enough, the assistant says it is not confident instead of guessing and lists the closest passages. Try rephrasing with specific words from the topic, or ask an administrator to add the missing guidance to the knowledge base. A low-confidence answer is a signal that the knowledge base needs a new entry."),
    ("What the assistant cannot do", G, "The assistant cannot approve donations, verify organizations, make decisions about children or give legal advice. It only explains, suggests and shows data. Volunteers receive policy answers only and no live data. Every suggestion is for an administrator to review."),
    ("Questions to ask the assistant about live data", G, "Administrators can ask which needs are most urgent and which donors to select, compare all organizations, ask which donors have stopped giving, or name an organization, donor or need category for a focused analysis. Organizations can ask how to improve their health and development. Donors can ask what to support first. Short follow-ups such as and for Little Stars work too."),
    ("How to add knowledge entries for the assistant", G, "Administrators and organizations can add knowledge entries in the assistant page: a title, the guidance text and a source. Write one topic per entry in plain sentences and include the words people would use when asking. After saving, entries are indexed so the assistant can find them. Organization entries are visible only to that organization and administrators."),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ safeguarding templates
    ("Child safeguarding principles (template)", T, "Every child has the right to be safe. Adapt these principles to your organization's own child protection policy and to local law. The child's welfare comes first. All adults working with children must be screened and trained. Concerns are always taken seriously and reported, never ignored. Information about a child is shared only with people who need it."),
    ("How to report a safeguarding concern (template)", T, "If you suspect a child is at risk, act immediately: make sure the child is safe, write down what you saw or heard in facts, tell your designated safeguarding lead, and follow your legal reporting duties. In India the national child helpline CHILDLINE is 1098 and the emergency number is 112; check the numbers that apply where you work. Do not investigate yourself and do not promise secrecy."),
    ("Volunteer screening and background checks (template)", T, "Before a volunteer works with children, collect identification, check references, run the background check required by your policy and local law, and give safeguarding training. Volunteers should not be alone with a child, and should follow the code of conduct. Record the checks. The platform does not perform checks for you."),
    ("Photos and media consent (template)", T, "Do not take or share photos or videos of children without written consent from the person responsible for the child and following your policy. Never publish a child's full name with a photo, and never share images in public groups. Remove content on request."),
    ("Data retention and deletion (template)", T, "Keep personal data only as long as it is needed and as required by law. Review records on a schedule, remove data that is no longer needed, and respond to deletion requests according to your policy. Donations and matches are kept for the audit trail, so such records are closed rather than deleted."),
    ("What to do about a suspected fraudulent donation", G, "Treat a flagged donation as a question. Do not accuse the donor. Check the donor's history, the amount against their usual giving, and whether several gifts came very quickly. Contact the donor through known details, then record Confirm fraud or False alarm in the review queue. Keep notes in line with your finance policy."),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ troubleshooting, glossary, FAQ
    ("Troubleshooting sign-in problems", G, "If you cannot sign in, check these in order. Make sure you use the right sign-in page: administrators use the Administrator sign-in. Check the email spelling and that caps lock is off. If the message says too many attempts, wait 15 minutes. If you forgot the password use Forgot password. If your administrator account uses two-step verification, enter the 6-digit code or a recovery code, and check that your phone's clock is correct."),
    ("How to switch dark mode", G, "Use the sun and moon button in the top bar to switch between light and dark, or open Settings and choose Appearance to pick Light, Dark or System. System follows your device setting. Your choice is saved on this device."),
    ("Beginner glossary of terms", G, "Need: something a child or organization requires, like books or food. Donor: a person or group who gives. Volunteer: a person who gives time and skills. Resource: materials, services or funds that can be given. Allocation: assigning a resource to a need. Verified: an organization an administrator has checked. Flag: a warning raised by the AI for a person to check. Suggestion: an AI recommendation that a person approves or rejects. Audit trail: the permanent record of actions."),
    ("What AI and machine learning mean here", G, "AI in CareBridge means programs that look at data and make suggestions. Some are fixed rules that anyone can read, such as the fraud checks. Some are machine learning models that learn patterns, such as the career model and the donor and volunteer models that learn from administrator decisions. None of them decides alone: a person reviews every important result."),
    ("Can the AI approve things automatically", G, "No. The AI only recommends, flags and ranks. Verification, fraud decisions, donor and volunteer matches, resource allocations and need alerts all wait in the review queue for an administrator. This keeps people accountable and lets mistakes be caught."),
    ("How the system tries to be fair and explainable", G, "Every suggestion shows its score and the reasons behind it, so an administrator can see why a donor, volunteer or resource was ranked high. Rejected suggestions are not repeated and administrator decisions are recorded. Because people review each result, the AI's mistakes or unfairness can be noticed and corrected. Check suggestions for unfair patterns regularly."),
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ AI methods (administrators)
    ("How the AI agent works", G, "The AI agent reads a question, decides which tools it needs, runs them and explains the result. Retrieval tools read the knowledge base and the live database. Machine-learning tools forecast demand, predict whether a need will be fulfilled, and spot unusual records. Python tools do exact arithmetic. Optimization tools plan allocations and matches under constraints. Only administrators can use the machine-learning and optimization tools. The agent never approves anything: its plans are proposals that wait in the review queue, and every run is recorded in the audit trail."),
    ("How predictions are evaluated", G, "The platform predicts one thing: whether a need will be fulfilled within 14 days of being posted. It starts with a simple baseline, the fulfilment rate of each urgency level, then compares logistic regression, random forest, gradient boosting, XGBoost, support vector machine and nearest neighbours. A model replaces the baseline only if it beats it in cross-validation by a clear margin. The final score is measured on the most recent 20 percent of needs, which the models never saw, and is shown with a confidence interval. At least 80 labelled needs are required; until then the baseline is used. Results on the built-in synthetic benchmark describe the method, not real-world accuracy."),
    ("How the optimizer allocates resources fairly", G, "The optimizer solves a linear or mixed-integer program. Each unit of stock can go to a need only up to the stock on hand and the amount still needed. Critical needs are served first, then fairness is applied: max-min fairness raises the coverage of the worst-served need as far as possible, while a fairness floor guarantees each need a share of that best value. An optional cap limits how much any one organization can receive. The plan is compared with a simple greedy plan, checked independently against every limit, and is only ever a proposal that an administrator approves."),
    ("How the demand forecast is checked", G, "Each category's monthly number of needs is forecast with several methods: the last value, a three-month average, damped-trend smoothing and ARIMA. They are scored by rolling backtests, repeatedly forecasting one month ahead from past data and measuring the error. The method with the lowest error is used, but never one that is worse than simply repeating the last value. With fewer than six months of history no backtest is possible and the forecast is marked rough. A deep-learning LSTM model is not used because there is too little data."),
    ("What the data quality check verifies", G, "The data quality check looks for broken links between records, such as a donation pointing to a missing need, impossible values such as negative quantities, and inventory that does not add up: available stock must equal the total minus approved and delivered allocations. It also finds over-allocated needs, duplicate donors and needs, organizations whose verified flag and status disagree, and scores that fall outside their documented ranges. Safe problems such as inventory counts can be repaired in one click; everything else is reported for a person to fix."),
    ("How retrieval GraphRAG and corrective RAG work", G, "To answer a question the assistant first routes it to the right source, splits multi-part questions, and applies topic filters. It searches by words, by spelling-tolerant text and by meaning, fuses the rankings, adds a knowledge-graph score that follows related concepts, and reranks the best passages. If the first retrieval is weak it relaxes the filter and rewrites the query, but confidence is always judged against the original question. Answers quote the passages with numbered citations, each sentence is checked against its source, and numbers that are not in the evidence are rejected."),
    ("What the AI Lab page shows", G, "The AI Lab page lets administrators ask the AI agent questions and see which tools it used, in what order, with every result. It shows the prediction model status and lets you train it when enough real history exists, the demand forecasts with their backtests, unusual donations and needs, and donor segments. It runs the resource allocation and donor matching optimizers with fairness settings and can add the plans to the review queue as proposals. It also shows the data quality check and the stored benchmark report."),
    ("How often to run scans and review the queue", G, "A good routine is to run all AI scans once a day, clear the review queue, and look at the audit trail and the organization health scores weekly. Critical needs and suspicious donation flags should be handled the same day."),
]

assert len({e[0] for e in ENTRIES}) == len(ENTRIES), "duplicate knowledge-base titles"


